 # Volume Raycasting Discretization
 
-Also called: CPU Ray Casting (Front-to-Back), Discrete Volume Rendering Integral, Fixed-step Raycasting, Ray Marching, Volume ray marching
+Also called: CPU Ray Casting (Front-to-Back), Discrete Volume Rendering Integral, Fixed-step Raycasting, Volume ray marching
 
 ## Overview
-Volume Raycasting Discretization is a direct volume rendering technique that approximates the volume rendering integral by sampling a 3D scalar field at fixed intervals along rays cast from the camera [S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of mapping volumetric data to a 2D image plane by performing front-to-back alpha compositing of sampled color and opacity values [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its use of a fixed step size and explicit discretization of the continuous rendering integral, often implemented on the CPU for simplicity or educational purposes, contrasting with GPU-based ray marching which may use variable step sizes or hierarchical acceleration structures [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Volume Raycasting Discretization is a direct volume rendering technique that approximates the volume rendering integral by sampling a 3D scalar field at fixed intervals along rays cast from the camera [S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of mapping volumetric data to a 2D image plane by performing front-to-back alpha compositing of sampled color and opacity values [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its explicit discretization of the continuous rendering integral into a fixed-step summation, typically implemented on the CPU for simplicity or as a baseline [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
